From 7a9a1a0eab93b99affeed4ea4362aa71582bdd4a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: x <x@example.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 16:38:03 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(tui): owner-addressed notifications wait with their
 owner, not on the shared queue [daveey]

Every live chat's poller drains one process-wide completion queue. An event whose owner was
mid-turn went back on that queue, so every other poller dequeued it, resolved its compression
lineage against state.db and re-queued it, ~10x/s each, for the whole turn. On a 22-chat
Desktop backend with one long autonomous turn this pinned the backend at 300% CPU, stalled
its event loop 5-13 s and timed out every Desktop request (2026-10-27).

- Hand a foreign event to its owner's poller (session["_notif_held"]); a busy owner holds
  its own addressed events and retries them when idle; the shutdown drain returns them to
  the shared queue. Ownerless events and owners without a poller keep the old route.
- kanban_session_activity._all_counts is single flight: readers queued behind a recompute
  take its result (the fingerprint moves on every kanban write, so ~24 sidebar requests
  recomputed one after another behind _pairs_lock).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
